-### Review a runtime-role template at plan time
-
-`print(plan.runtime_role_template("cryptalis_runtime_new"))` prints a transactional
-CREATE ROLE and declared-table GRANT template for a fresh NOLOGIN role. It executes
-nothing. An administrator reviews and runs it separately; login assignment and existing
-PUBLIC/membership rights remain explicit deployment responsibilities. See
-[the lifecycle template contract](docs/lifecycle.md#print-only-runtime-role-setup-template).
-
-### Explicit offline deprotection candidate
-
-`cryptalis.deprotection.plan` takes the current protect plan, its independently
-current ACTIVE deployment pin, the maintenance engine, admitted keys and measured
-cost estimates. It reads only. `DeprotectionPlan` binds this source in a versioned
-reverse artifact; do not substitute database journal contents for external authority.
-
-`apply` requires both `MaintenanceApproval` and an operation-bound
-`PlaintextApproval`. The latter explicitly accepts plaintext in live storage,
-WAL, replicas and backups and names retained encrypted-backup recovery ownership.
-The default stops at `PLAINTEXT_PENDING`; only a matching external
-`PLAINTEXT_ACTIVATING` pin allows `PLAINTEXT_ACTIVE`. Writers stay fenced before
-activation. Every switch repeats full current-ciphertext versus staged-text
-verification. Data and its progress marker commit in the same PostgreSQL
-transaction; external publication is a separate fail-closed handoff.
-
-`abort` is pre-switch only and preserves current encrypted values. `remove`
-requires an external REMOVING pin and `RemovalApproval` identifying executed
-package-free workload and retained-backup evidence. It retires only inventoried
-operation metadata, including explicitly bound aborted reverse operations.
-Unreviewed journal rows or dependencies refuse removal. These evidence references
-are host attestations, not automatic verification of the host's backup service.
-After publishing REMOVED, retain the reverse artifact and run `check_removed`
-through a trusted deployment/restore gate outside the package-free application.
-
-Limits: native text only; maintenance downtime; no erasure of historical bytes;
-no production provider custody qualification. Named-column values, NULL behavior,
-collation and admitted indexes/uniqueness are restored. Physical column ordinals
-can change, so positional `SELECT *`/COPY consumers are not qualified; use explicit
-column lists while the original-order preservation requirement remains open.
-A structurally identical old plaintext snapshot is not detected by schema checks
-alone: external backup provenance and restore quarantine remain required.
-SQLModel/VARCHAR and full framework retrofit demonstrations are separate slices.
